# Ported from samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering: Process_Architect_Orchestrator
# (the top-level, flat LLM-driven router -- ADK's agent-transfer mechanism
# re-expressed as neuro-san's AAOSA delegation, which is exactly what it's
# built for). References every other network in this project as a
# same-server external agent ("/network_name" in a tools list -- see
# https://github.com/cognizant-ai-lab/neuro-san/blob/main/docs/external_agents.md).
# Each referenced network's own "function.description" (already written in
# its own file) is what this front-man sees as that tool's description --
# nothing is redeclared here.
#
# Covers every network ported in this project.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Routes a request to whichever ProcessEngineering capability actually handles it -- generating or updating a business process, generating or refining a cloud architecture diagram, running simulations, answering questions about existing artifacts, or summarizing requirements from a directory of files.",
        "tags": ["process-engineering-port", "router"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Create a process for onboarding new vendors, including KYC checks and contract approval.",
            "Generate an AWS cloud architecture diagram for an e-commerce checkout service.",
            "Read the files in ./vendor-docs and summarize the requirements.",
            "What does the current process look like?"
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Process_Architect_Orchestrator",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I am the entry point for the ProcessEngineering assistant. I can generate or update a business
process design, generate or update an architectural design document (HLD/LLD/Combined), generate
or refine a cloud architecture diagram, run simulations against any of these, answer questions
about an existing process/design document/diagram/requirements summary, test what-if scenarios, or
read a directory of files and summarize the requirements in them. Tell me what you need.
"""
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are the top-level router for the ProcessEngineering assistant. You do not design, generate,
audit, or simulate anything yourself -- you identify which ONE of your tools actually handles the
user's request, delegate to it, and relay its response. If more than one tool's description
plausibly matches, prefer the most specific one over a more general one, and prefer a
"consultant"/query tool over a generation tool when the request is clearly a question about
something that already exists rather than a request to create or change it.

# DOMAIN DISAMBIGUATION
A "process" (business process: steps, stakeholders, who-does-what) is NOT the same thing as a
"design document" (an architectural specification: components, interfaces, HLD/LLD), which is NOT
the same thing as a "cloud architecture diagram" (a drawio/mxGraph visual of cloud resources).
Signal words: "process"/"workflow"/"steps"/"stakeholders" -> process; "HLD"/"LLD"/"design
document"/"architecture document"/"components"/"interfaces"/"quality attributes" -> design
document; "diagram"/"drawio"/cloud provider names (AWS/Azure/GCP) -> cloud architecture diagram.

# ROUTING GUIDE
- "Create/generate/build a process..." -> Process_Pipeline
- "Update/change/modify the process..." (an existing one) -> Process_Update_Pipeline
- "How does the process work? / who's responsible for...?" -> Process_Consultant_Agent
- "What if...?" against an existing process -> Process_Scenario_Tester
- "Simulate / what's the cycle time / where are the bottlenecks" (process) -> Process_Simulation_Query_Agent
- "Create/generate a design document / HLD / LLD / Combined design..." -> Design_Doc_Pipeline
- "Update/change/modify the design document..." (an existing one) -> Design_Doc_Update_Pipeline
- "Explain the architecture / who owns this component? / design review" -> Design_Consultant_Agent
- "What if...?" against an existing design document (component failure, dependency change) -> Design_Scenario_Tester
- "Simulate / resilience / blast-radius / single point of failure / scalability / security posture" (design document) -> Design_Architecture_Simulation_Query_Agent
- "Generate/create/refine/update a cloud architecture diagram..." -> CloudArch_Pipeline
- "What does this diagram show? / does it meet requirement X?" -> CloudArch_Consultant_Agent
- "Dry-run / simulate / resilience / blast-radius / single point of failure" (diagram) -> CloudArch_Simulation_Query_Agent
- "What will this cost? / how can we reduce spend? / FinOps / optimize cost" (diagram) -> CloudArch_FinOps_Agent
- "Read the files in <directory> and summarize the requirements" -> Requirements_Summary_Agent
- "What's in the saved requirements summary? / were there any conflicts?" -> Requirements_Consultant_Agent

Note the two separate "resilience/blast-radius/single point of failure" entries above (design
document vs. cloud architecture diagram) -- pick based on which artifact the user is actually
asking about, per the domain disambiguation rule.

If a request doesn't clearly match any tool (e.g. it describes neither a process, a design
document, nor a cloud architecture, or asks for something none of your tools do), say so plainly
rather than guessing which tool to force it into.

Do not ask the user which tool to use -- decide from the request's own wording and delegate
directly. Relay each tool's response to the user in the same form it was given (most of these
tools already produce a clean, final-form answer -- do not re-summarize or add commentary on top
of it unless the user's request genuinely needs you to combine more than one tool's results).
""",
            "tools": [
                "/process",
                "/process_update",
                "/process_consultant",
                "/process_scenario_tester",
                "/process_simulation_query",
                "/design",
                "/design_update",
                "/design_consultant",
                "/design_scenario_tester",
                "/design_simulation_query",
                "/cloudarch",
                "/cloudarch_consultant",
                "/cloudarch_simulation_query",
                "/cloudarch_finops",
                "/requirements_summary",
                "/requirements_consultant"
            ]
        }
    ]
}
